<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elizabeth Stuart, Queen of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1596">1596</a>–<a href="/years/1662">1662</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Winter Queen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elizabeth Stuart was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a> and the wife of Frederick V, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, whom she married in 1613. Her husband's acceptance of the Bohemian crown made her queen for a single winter in 1619–1620, hence her name, the Winter Queen, and the couple spent their exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>. Through her daughter Sophia, electress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, the British throne passed to the house of Hanover in 1714.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elizabeth-stuart-queen-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Elizabeth Stuart, Queen of Bohemia" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Michiel Jansz. van Mierevelt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Workshop_of_Michiel_Jansz._van_Miereveldt_Elizabeth_Stuart_Queen_of_Bohemia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Elizabeth Stuart was born at Dunfermline Palace on 19 August 1596, the only surviving daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a> and Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and the elder sister of Charles I. She was named for her godmother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. After 1603 she was brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, chiefly under Lord and Lady Harington at Coombe Abbey.</p><p>Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder-plot">Gunpowder Plot</a> of 1605 was a scheme to kidnap her, place her on the throne as a puppet and raise her as a Catholic, a plan that failed when the plotters were betrayed. Her education covered natural history, geography, theology, languages, music and dancing, but not the classics, because her father thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> made women cunning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Many suitors sought her hand, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. A marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-spain">Philip III of Spain</a> would have meant conversion, which the English court would not accept. The man chosen was Frederick V, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, a senior prince of the Empire and a staunch Protestant. He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 16 October 1612.</p><p>Her brother Henry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, died on 6 November 1612, and the wedding took place on 14 February 1613 at the Palace of Whitehall, with festivities that cost nearly £50,000 and almost bankrupted the king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-donne">John Donne</a> wrote a marriage song for the occasion. At Heidelberg Frederick built the Englischer Bau for her and began the Hortus Palatinus, laid out by her former tutor Salomon de Caus. She bore three children there.</p><p>In 1619 the Bohemian nobles deposed the Catholic Ferdinand and offered the crown to Frederick. Elizabeth urged him to accept. He was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St Vitus Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 4 November 1619, and she was crowned three days later. Their reign ended with the defeat of the Bohemian Protestant armies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> on 8 November 1620, which ended the first phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and they became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Winter King</a> and Queen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Elizabeth left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> before the defeat and bore her son Maurice at Custrin on 6 January 1621. The Palatinate was occupied, and at the invitation of the Prince of Orange the family settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, where she bore eight more children. Frederick died on 29 November 1632. Elizabeth stayed at The Hague even after her son Charles Louis regained the Palatinate in 1648, and she worked to arrange marriages for her children.</p><p>After the Restoration she reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 26 May 1661. She died of pneumonia at Leicester House on 13 February 1662 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>. When Queen Anne died in 1714, the last Stuart monarch, the British throne passed to Elizabeth's grandson George I, son of her daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-of-hanover">Sophia of Hanover</a>, who began the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elizabeth Stuart, Queen of Bohemia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The marriage of Frederick V to <b>Elizabeth Stuart</b>, daughter of James VI and I, in 1613 tied German Protestantism to Britain and the Dutch Republic, and through their daughter Sophia, electress of Hanover, the British throne passed to the house of Hanover in 1714.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1638" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Elizabeth Stuart, Queen of Bohemia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1638">What was here in 1638? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
